.TH "mbedded\-cbor\-runtime" "7" "2026-11-08" "mbedded\-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded\-cbor"
.SH Run\-time API
\f[CR]cbor::lazy\f[R] reads CBOR step by step.
It reads only the parts that you ask for, and a text comes back as a
view into the bytes, with no copy.
basics.md states how errors come back and how long a result stays valid.
The reference with examples that run in the browser:
https://asmod4n.github.io/mbedded\-cbor/lazy.html and
https://asmod4n.github.io/mbedded\-cbor/item.html.
.SS 1. Read step by step
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: {\(dqcars\(dq: [{\(dqhp\(dq: 5}, {\(dqhp\(dq: 7}]}\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const hp = cbor::lazy::take_ownership(std::move(bytes))
                    .and_then([](cbor::lazy const &doc) { \f[B]return\f[R] doc.at(\(dqcars\(dq); })
                    .and_then([](cbor::lazy const &cars) { \f[B]return\f[R] cars.at(1); })
                    .and_then([](cbor::lazy const &car) { \f[B]return\f[R] car.at(\(dqhp\(dq); })
                    .and_then([](cbor::lazy const &h) { \f[B]return\f[R] h.get<std::int64_t>(); });
\f[B]if\f[R] (hp)
    use(*hp);   \f[I]// 7\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]cbor::lazy::take_ownership(std::move(bytes))\f[R] makes a lazy, a
position in the bytes.
Bytes go into the library in three forms, and no form copies them:
.PP
