.PP
A \f[CR]cbor::ref\f[R] views the bytes and keeps their owner alive.
Bind it to a variable before you read it: \f[CR]*\f[R] and
\f[CR]\->\f[R] of a temporary \f[CR]cbor::ref\f[R] do not compile.
.PP
Another kind of value gives \f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R].
A number that does not fit \f[CR]T\f[R] gives
\f[CR]number_out_of_range\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: {\(dqname\(dq: \(dqann\(dq, \(dqage\(dq: 300}\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const doc = cbor::lazy::take_ownership(std::move(bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const name = doc\->at(\(dqname\(dq)\->get<std::string_view>();   \f[I]// **name == \(dqann\(dq\f[R]
doc\->at(\(dqage\(dq)\->get<std::int64_t>();                          \f[I]// 300\f[R]
doc\->at(\(dqage\(dq)\->get<std::uint8_t>().error();                  \f[I]// number_out_of_range\f[R]
doc\->at(\(dqname\(dq)\->get<std::int64_t>().error();                 \f[I]// incorrect_type\f[R]
.EE
.SS 3. Loop over an array or a map
\f[CR]elements()\f[R] gives the elements of an array, and
\f[CR]entries()\f[R] gives the pairs of a map.
Each element is a \f[CR]std::expected\f[R] of its own.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: [1, 2, 3]\f[R]
std::expected<std::int64_t, cbor::error> sum(std::string bytes)
{
    \f[B]auto\f[R] const array = cbor::lazy::take_ownership(std::move(bytes));
    \f[B]if\f[R] (!array)
        \f[B]return\f[R] std::unexpected(array.error());
    \f[B]auto\f[R] const elements = array\->elements();
    \f[B]if\f[R] (!elements)
        \f[B]return\f[R] std::unexpected(elements.error());
    std::int64_t total = 0;
    \f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]auto\f[R] const element : *elements) {
        \f[B]if\f[R] (!element)
            \f[B]return\f[R] std::unexpected(element.error());
        \f[B]auto\f[R] const n = element\->get<std::int64_t>();
        \f[B]if\f[R] (!n)
            \f[B]return\f[R] std::unexpected(n.error());
        total += *n;
    }
    \f[B]return\f[R] total;   \f[I]// 6\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
A pair of a map has the key in \f[CR]first\f[R] and the value in
\f[CR]second\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: {\(dqa\(dq: 1, \(dqb\(dq: 2}\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const map = cbor::lazy::take_ownership(std::move(bytes));
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]auto\f[R] const pair : *map\->entries())
    \f[B]if\f[R] (pair)
        use(pair\->first.get<std::string_view>(), pair\->second.get<std::int64_t>());   \f[I]// \(dqa\(dq 1, then \(dqb\(dq 2\f[R]
.EE
.SS 4. Size and keys
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: {\(dqa\(dq: 1, \(dqb\(dq: 2}\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const map = cbor::lazy::take_ownership(std::move(bytes));
map\->size();          \f[I]// 2, the pairs of a map or the elements of an array\f[R]
map\->at(\(dqz\(dq).error(); \f[I]// key_not_found, the key is not in the map\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Both return a \f[CR]std::expected\f[R].
.SS 5. Print CBOR as text
\f[CR]cbor::diagnostic_notation(bytes)\f[R] gives the CBOR as text, in
the diagnostic notation of RFC 8949.
It looks like JSON.
Use it to look at input in a log or in a test.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: the CBOR of section 1\f[R]
cbor::diagnostic_notation(bytes);   \f[I]// the text {\(dqcars\(dq: [{\(dqhp\(dq: 5}, {\(dqhp\(dq: 7}]}\f[R]
.EE
.PP
It returns a \f[CR]std::expected<std::string, cbor::error>\f[R].
Bytes that are not CBOR give an error.
.SS 6. Write CBOR
\f[CR]cbor::structs<T>::encode\f[R] writes a C++ value as CBOR, on every
compiler (structs.md).
\f[CR]cbor::encoder\f[R] writes one item per call:
.IP
.EX
std::string out;
cbor::encoder e(out);
e.map(1);
e.text_string(\(dqa\(dq);
e.unsigned_integer(std::uint8_t{5});
e.finish();   \f[I]// out holds a1 61 61 18 05, {\(dqa\(dq: 5_0}\f[R]
.EE
.PP
An integer takes the width of its type, so \f[CR]std::uint8_t{5}\f[R]
takes a head of two bytes.
.SS 7. Read items from a socket
A socket or a pipe gives bytes in pieces.
A piece can end in the middle of an item.
\f[CR]cbor::sequence_reader\f[R] reads a CBOR sequence (RFC 8742): items
one after the other, with nothing between them.
.IP
.EX
cbor::sequence_reader r;
r.feed(std::move(chunk));         \f[I]// a std::string that arrived\f[R]
\f[B]while\f[R] (\f[B]auto\f[R] item = r.next())      \f[I]// each item that is complete\f[R]
    handle(*item);                \f[I]// a cbor::lazy\f[R]
\f[I]// r.next().error() == cbor::error::too_little_data: wait for more bytes\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]feed\f[R] takes a \f[CR]std::string\f[R] that you move in without
a copy, and a \f[CR]std::shared_ptr<std::string const>\f[R] without a
copy.
It copies a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] and a \f[CR]std::string\f[R]
that you do not move.
\f[CR]next\f[R] gives one item when all of its bytes are there.
It gives \f[CR]too_little_data\f[R] while it waits.
The reader continues where it stopped.
.PP
Each item is a \f[CR]cbor::lazy\f[R] that keeps its bytes alive.
You can keep it after later calls of \f[CR]feed\f[R] and after the
reader is gone.
The reader copies only an item that a piece cuts.
.PP
Bytes that are not CBOR give their error, for example
\f[CR]syntax_error\f[R].
After such an error every call gives the same error, and \f[CR]feed\f[R]
keeps no byte, because RFC 8742 gives no way to find the start of the
next item.
.PP
At the end of the stream, \f[CR]r.empty()\f[R] tells you whether the
reader still holds bytes of an item that is not complete.
.PP
The reader refuses an item nested deeper than \f[CR]cbor::limits\f[R]
allows.
The other limits apply when you read the item.
Bound the bytes that you give to the reader.
.PP
